            {
              "id": "glog-2793ef3a-21b7-402e-8f80-93c2d524d33e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. The specific vulnerability in this case is related to the casting of `k` to `size_t` before passing it to `malloc`. If `k` is a negative integer, casting it to `size_t` (which is an unsigned type) will result in a very large positive number. This can lead to an attempt to allocate a large amount of memory, potentially causing a buffer overflow or out-of-memory error.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the value of `k` before casting it to `size_t` and passing it to `malloc`. If `k` is negative, you should handle this case appropriately, for example by returning an error or setting `k` to zero.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nif (k < 0) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    malloc(static_cast<size_t>(k));\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if `k` is negative. If it is, we handle the error (the exact handling depends on your specific application). If `k` is not negative, we proceed with the `malloc` call.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so you don't need any additional libraries to use it in C++.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to the CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size. You can find more information about this weakness on the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)."
              },
