            {
              "id": "glog-9bb3e695-175a-4a21-8a6b-eb9e2671ab5a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\nThe specific sink in question `malloc(static_cast<size_t>(n))` is vulnerable if the value of `n` is not properly validated or controlled. If `n` is a negative number, the `static_cast<size_t>` will convert it to a very large positive number, potentially leading to a buffer overflow or memory corruption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes:\n\n- Checking that the size is not negative before casting it to `size_t`.\n- Checking that the size does not exceed the maximum allowable size.\n- Handling the case where `malloc` returns `NULL`, which indicates that the memory allocation failed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nif (n < 0) {\n    // Handle error: size cannot be negative\n} else if (n > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: size exceeds maximum allowable size\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(static_cast<size_t>(n));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr...\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nReplace `MAX_SIZE` with the maximum allowable size for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
