            {
              "id": "glog-2c2d843e-dd93-4e5a-b8c7-d3013908fbdf",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The problem arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is calculated using user-supplied input. If the user supplies a large value, it can lead to an integer overflow during the calculation. This overflow can result in a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate user-supplied input and avoid using it directly in memory allocation functions like `malloc`. You should also consider using functions that can handle memory allocation errors more gracefully, such as `calloc`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `malloc(n * k * ho * wo * data_byte)`, consider using `calloc` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t total_size = n * k * ho * wo;\nif(total_size / n / k / ho / wo != data_byte) {\n    // handle overflow error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = calloc(total_size, data_byte);\n    if(!ptr) {\n        // handle allocation error\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis code checks for an integer overflow before allocating memory and uses `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero and can handle allocation errors more gracefully.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed for the code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
