            {
              "id": "glog-2668b74e-7d26-4c4c-aec9-b81ed6503313",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is specified in bytes. The problem arises when the size calculation overflows, which can lead to an allocation of a smaller block of memory than expected. This can lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability if the program later writes more data to the block than it can hold.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of your size calculations and the return value of `malloc`. If the size calculation overflows or `malloc` returns `NULL`, you should handle these error conditions appropriately. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// Check for overflow in size calculation\nif (n > 0 && c > 0 && hi > 0 && wi > 0 && data_byte > 0 &&\n    c <= INT_MAX / n && \n    hi <= INT_MAX / (n * c) && \n    wi <= INT_MAX / (n * c * hi) && \n    data_byte <= INT_MAX / (n * c * hi * wi)) {\n    size_t size = n * c * hi * wi * data_byte;\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    } else {\n        // Use allocated memory\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle size calculation overflow\n}\n```\n\nThis code first checks if the size calculation would overflow. If it would, it handles this as an error condition. If the size calculation does not overflow, it calls `malloc` and checks if the allocation was successful. If it was not, it handles this as an error condition.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
