            {
              "id": "glog-70c3438d-fc50-4c8e-b5a3-908434f133dd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled. This can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\nThe specific sink in question `malloc(static_cast<size_t>(n))` is vulnerable if the value of `n` is not properly validated or controlled. If `n` is a negative number, the `static_cast<size_t>` will convert it to a very large positive number, potentially leading to a buffer overflow or memory corruption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated. This includes:\n\n- Checking that the size is not negative before casting it to `size_t`.\n- Checking that the size does not exceed the maximum allowable size.\n- Handling the case where `malloc` returns `NULL`, which indicates that the memory allocation failed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nif (n < 0) {\n    // Handle error: size cannot be negative\n} else if (n > MAX_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error: size exceeds maximum allowable size\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(static_cast<size_t>(n));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: memory allocation failed\n    } else {\n        // Use ptr...\n        free(ptr);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nReplace `MAX_SIZE` with the maximum allowable size for your specific use case.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-e20e6f5e-1c8c-4b91-947c-b96cbef6a671",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. The specific vulnerability in this case is that the size of the memory to be allocated is not checked before the `malloc` function is called. This can lead to a number of issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or even denial of service if an attacker can control the value of `k`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate the size of the memory to be allocated before calling `malloc`. This includes checking that the size is not negative and that it does not exceed the maximum allowable size. You should also handle the case where `malloc` fails to allocate the requested memory, which can happen if there is not enough free memory available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for malloc and free\n#include <climits> // for SIZE_MAX\n\n// ...\n\nif (k > 0 && k <= SIZE_MAX) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(static_cast<size_t>(k));\n    if (ptr == nullptr) {\n        // handle malloc failure\n    } else {\n        // use ptr\n        free(ptr); // don't forget to free the memory when you're done\n    }\n} else {\n    // handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header file is required for the `malloc` and `free` functions.\n- `<climits>`: This header file is required for the `SIZE_MAX` constant.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-dbe60730-b1b4-4e10-b1f4-0523088d85b8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if `karg_size` is larger than the size of `karg_buffer`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution. An attacker can exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the data and the size of the buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\nAdditionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy` such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` which include a size parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (karg_size <= sizeof(karg_buffer)) {\n    memcpy(static_cast<void*>(&karg_buffer[0]), static_cast<void*>(&karg), karg_size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we check if `karg_size` is less than or equal to the size of `karg_buffer` before calling `memcpy`. If `karg_size` is larger, we handle the error appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `memcpy` function.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
